---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — On the fault"
  description="On-fault Coulomb excess, stress changes and slip on three rough faults that break the free surface, one of them a splay rooted on another, one value per element, with a mollified kernel and with a classical triangular dislocation element."
  current="onfault"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>On the fault<span class="sub">one value per element</span></h1>
    <p>
      Three rough faults that break the surface, two of them joined. A is the
      main 30&deg; thrust; B a parallel ramp 3&nbsp;km into its hanging wall;
      C a 50&deg; splay rooted on A along a line the two meshes share. Each
      element is painted with one value: the one read at its centroid, where
      the frictional solve enforced its condition.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two kernels, one solve, one window: the mollified kernel of this package
      and the classical triangular dislocation element. Both are finite at a
      centroid, and away from the surface they give the same solve to a per
      cent. Where they part is the row of elements that touch the surface
      &mdash; the last section below.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      Colour is <b>data</b>: one value per element, nothing interpolated, no
      light. The grey sheet is the free surface; the magenta loops are the
      rims, each trace and the junction chord included.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The legend states the saturated area at the current window for both
      kernels at once; move the window and watch which one moves.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="onfault">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="kernel"><span>kernel</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="metric"><span>metric</span></label>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="window"><span class="lbl">window</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="Three rough faults breaking the free surface, one a splay rooted on another, coloured by on-fault Coulomb excess, one value per element, for the mollified and the classical kernel"></div>
    <p data-legend class="legend"></p>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mark"><span>mark saturated</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="wire"><span>element edges</span></label>
      <span class="lbl">view</span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="view"></span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="shot"></span>
      <label data-ctl="publication"><span>publication view</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system</h2>
  <p>
    Three rough discs, each cut by the free surface, at
    <i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.25&nbsp;km and
    <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.025&nbsp;km, under
    the published thrust load with friction 0.6 and an effective normal stress
    of 150&nbsp;MPa:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>fault</th><th>radius</th><th>dip</th><th>trace</th><th>elements</th><th>role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>6 km</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>8.9 km</td><td>3770</td>
            <td>the main thrust</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>4 km</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>5.3 km</td><td>1730</td>
            <td>a parallel ramp 3 km into A&rsquo;s hanging wall, where A&rsquo;s slip shadows it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>4 km</td><td>50&deg;</td><td>5.5 km</td><td>1558</td>
            <td>a splay rooted on A at 4.5 km depth, misoriented for this load</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    7058 elements. Every fault strikes along <i>y</i> and dips toward
    +<i>x</i>; its trace is where the plane meets <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, and
    the disc&rsquo;s centre sits 4, 3 and 2.9&nbsp;km down-dip of it, so each
    circle is cut by the surface and the trace is a straight chord whose
    vertices lie exactly on <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. C is cut twice: by the
    surface, and by the line where its plane meets A&rsquo;s, 7.8&nbsp;km from
    A&rsquo;s trace, 4.5&nbsp;km deep, 5.5&nbsp;km long, at 20&deg; to A.
    That chord is C&rsquo;s lower boundary and an interior line of A&rsquo;s
    mesh at once. Its 23 vertices are computed once and written into both
    meshes, so A&rsquo;s edges along it are C&rsquo;s boundary edges and the
    two tilings share them bit for bit; <i>conformal</i> here is a statement
    about the floats, and the selfcheck pins it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The outline of each fault is sampled on its circle and its chords at a
    spacing of <i>h</i>, and the interior is a constrained Delaunay
    triangulation (Shewchuk&rsquo;s Triangle) with no point added on any
    segment, so every rim vertex lies on its curve and the median edge is
    1.00&nbsp;<i>h</i> on all three faults. The roughness is the published
    field &mdash; rms facet slope 0.25, correlation length 4&nbsp;km &mdash;
    displaced along each fault&rsquo;s normal and tapered to zero within
    1.5&nbsp;km of a rim or a trace and within 3&nbsp;km of the junction, so a
    rim vertex stays on its curve, a trace vertex on the surface and a junction
    vertex shared. A and B keep the published tilt (rms 14.0&deg; and
    14.9&deg;); C, 5.8&nbsp;km wide between its two chords, is tapered over
    most of its width and tilts 8.9&deg;. Off the junction, C comes within
    53&nbsp;m of A, two <i>eps</i>; B&rsquo;s plane is 3&nbsp;km from
    A&rsquo;s; no other pair comes within 0.5&nbsp;km.
  </p>

  <h2>The solve</h2>
  <p>
    The condition is Coulomb. At the load &lambda; the solve finds the slip
    &delta; on every element such that each is either locked and below
    strength, or slipping and exactly at it:
    <i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&mu;</i>(<i>&sigma;</i><sub>eff,0</sub>&nbsp;+&nbsp;&lambda;<i>&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i><sup>load</sup>)&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;(&lambda;|<i>&tau;</i><sup>load</sup>|&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>P</i><sup>T</sup>&delta;)&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0,
    with &delta;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;<i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, a linear
    complementarity problem. The earlier version of this page coupled the
    slip-induced normal stress into the strength. With the splay rooted on A
    that coupling costs the operator its positive definiteness &mdash; the
    smallest eigenvalue of sym(&minus;<i>P</i><sub>eff</sub>) is &minus;1.4
    for the mollified kernel and &minus;2.7 for the classical one &mdash; so
    the solve here is the uncoupled problem for both kernels: each
    element&rsquo;s strength is set by the remote load alone, and the
    normal-stress change is read out but not fed back.
  </p>
  <p>
    The load is set by a rule, not by eye: the smallest multiple of the
    system&rsquo;s first failure at which A slips over at least 70&nbsp;% of
    its area and B and C are each partly locked and partly slipping. That is
    &lambda;&nbsp;=&nbsp;1.10&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;129&nbsp;MPa
    of tectonic shear:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>&lambda; / &lambda;<sub>0</sub></th><th>A active</th><th>B active</th><th>C active</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1.05</td><td>0.50</td><td>0.48</td><td>0.06</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1.10</b></td><td><b>0.81</b></td><td><b>0.82</b></td><td><b>0.11</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.20</td><td>0.97</td><td>0.93</td><td>0.17</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.35</td><td>1.00</td><td>0.98</td><td>0.23</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.50</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>0.28</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    At that load the remote shear on A is 105&nbsp;MPa against a strength of
    106; on C it is 123 against 155, which is what <i>misoriented</i> means
    for a 50&deg; plane under a load that is optimal at 30&deg;. Solving each
    fault <i>alone</i> on the same matrix says what the others do to it
    (mollified kernel):
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>fault</th><th>active alone</th><th>active in the system</th>
            <th>moment</th><th>Coulomb load from the others</th><th>max slip</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>0.82</td><td>0.81</td><td>&times;0.98</td>
            <td>&minus;0.09 MPa</td><td>0.78 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>0.83</td><td>0.82</td><td>&times;0.97</td>
            <td>&minus;0.11 MPa</td><td>0.92 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>0.11</td><td>0.11</td><td><b>&times;0.89</b></td>
            <td><b>&minus;1.18 MPa</b></td><td>0.21 m</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    B, 3&nbsp;km away, loses 3&nbsp;% of its moment to A&rsquo;s shadow; two
    kilometres closer, in the earlier version of this page, it lost a fifth. C
    loses 11&nbsp;%: A&rsquo;s slip unloads the splay above it by 1.2&nbsp;MPa,
    which on a fault 32&nbsp;MPa below strength keeps it locked everywhere but
    its deep end. The classical kernel gives the same active fractions and
    moment ratios to the second decimal. Its maximum slip on B is 1.12&nbsp;m
    against 0.92, and that difference is the subject of the last section.
  </p>

  <h2>What the maps show</h2>
  <p>
    The page opens on the Coulomb excess
    <i>S</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&tau;<sub>s</sub></i>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub>&prime;</i>
    at a window of &plusmn;79&nbsp;MPa, the 99th percentile of |<i>S</i>|
    over all elements. On a slipping element <i>S</i> is exactly zero &mdash;
    it is &minus;<i>w</i>, the solver&rsquo;s own margin, zero to
    10<sup>&minus;9</sup>&nbsp;GPa &mdash; and those are 66&nbsp;% of the area
    under both kernels, painted white. On a locked element it is the deficit
    below strength, painted blue: a median of 25&nbsp;MPa, a tenth of the
    locked elements more than 56, and 92 at the most, on the steepest facets
    of C, which under this load are the farthest from what it would take to
    move them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Switch the metric to the normal-stress change to see what the coupling
    would have fed back: slip on A clamps and unclamps its own rough surface by
    &minus;9 to +11&nbsp;MPa under the classical kernel, B by &minus;10 to +5,
    C by &minus;6 to +4; the window is &plusmn;7.7&nbsp;MPa. The shear change
    along the slip direction runs &minus;9 to +16&nbsp;MPa on A and &minus;9
    to +13 on B at a window of &plusmn;9. On a slipping element that shear is
    pinned by the friction law, <i>&tau;<sub>s</sub></i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub>&prime;</i>,
    so there the map is the remote deficit made up by slip; on a locked one it
    is what the neighbours transferred. The slip itself peaks at 0.78&nbsp;m
    on A, 0.92 on B and 0.21 on C under the mollified kernel.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the two kernels differ</h2>
  <p>
    On a slipping element the shear change is fixed by the friction law and
    the solve pins the slip to make it so, so on 66&nbsp;% of the area the two
    kernels&rsquo; shear maps are identical by construction. What is free to
    differ is the active set, the slip, the shear on locked elements and the
    normal-stress change everywhere. The active sets differ on 34 of the 7058
    elements. On locked elements the shear change differs by a median of
    0.08&nbsp;MPa and the normal-stress change by 0.07, with nothing above
    4&nbsp;MPa; below the first three rows of elements the normal-stress
    change agrees to 0.1&nbsp;MPa in the median on every fault.
  </p>
  <p>
    The row of elements that touch the surface is different. On A and B, the
    two 30&deg; faults, the mollified normal-stress change on that row reads
    up to +48&nbsp;MPa where the classical kernel reads &minus;5, a median
    difference of 6 and 8&nbsp;MPa on the two rows; on C, whose trace row is
    locked, the two agree to 0.2&nbsp;MPa. The same row slips about 40&nbsp;%
    less under the mollified kernel and the two rows below it a quarter to a
    third less, and on B, 4&nbsp;km across and 3.5&nbsp;km deep, that stiffer
    top carries through to a tenth less slip at depth and a classical moment
    17&nbsp;% larger than the mollified one, which is the 1.12&nbsp;m against
    0.92.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cause is where the smeared slip goes. A mollified element spreads its
    slip over <i>eps</i> on either side of its plane, and for an element that
    touches the surface that band straddles <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0: above the
    surface the image construction continues analytically, but there is no
    longer a free surface to enforce. The trace-row centroids sit
    2.5&nbsp;<i>eps</i> below the surface on B, and the error is
    <i>eps</i>&rsquo;s. The selfcheck solves B alone with the mollified
    matrix at <i>eps</i>/<i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1, 0.05 and 0.025 against the
    classical one: the classical-to-mollified slip ratio on the trace row
    falls <b>1.60, 1.20, 1.05</b>, and the whole fault&rsquo;s moment ratio
    1.17, 1.07, 1.02. Reading one fixed slip on that row with both kernels,
    the median normal-stress difference falls 7.3, 2.0, 0.5, 0.1&nbsp;MPa
    down to <i>eps</i>/<i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.0125, while three rows down it
    is 0.36&nbsp;MPa at the production <i>eps</i> already. First order in
    <i>eps</i>, as a smeared edge should be. So what the maps show for the
    mollified kernel at <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i> is the kernel
    at its resolution floor: <i>eps</i> is the length it cannot see below,
    and at a surface-breaking trace the free surface is inside that length.
    Solved at <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.025&nbsp;<i>h</i>, B&rsquo;s trace row
    agrees with the classical one to 5&nbsp;%.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs and windows</h2>
  <p>
    Traction is <i>&sigma;</i>&middot;<i>n</i> with each facet&rsquo;s upward
    normal, so its normal component is tension positive; the normal stress in
    the Coulomb strength is compression positive, which is the sign the
    friction module uses, and the two are pinned against each other rather
    than reasoned about. Every window is symmetric, &plusmn;<i>W</i>, with
    <i>W</i> the area-weighted 99th percentile of the <i>mollified</i> map
    rounded to two figures, and it is the same window for both kernels; the
    slider moves it and the legend counts what saturates. Nothing is smoothed,
    decimated or interpolated: each element carries the sample taken at its
    centroid, 0.29&nbsp;<i>h</i> from its edges on an equilateral element and
    never nearer than 1.3&nbsp;<i>eps</i> on this mesh.
  </p>
  <p>
    Until 16 October 2026 this page sampled 100 cells per facet to read the
    condition <i>between</i> the centroids, where the classical element
    diverges as 1/<i>d</i> and the mollified one stays bounded. That readout
    is still what <code>onfault.py field --k 10</code> produces; it is not
    what is shipped here.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Every array ships with its byte count and sha256 in
    <a href={`${base}data/onfault_manifest.json`}>onfault_manifest.json</a>,
    with the geometry of the junction, the load, the ladder it was chosen from,
    the window rule and both kernels&rsquo; definiteness, sweep counts and
    residuals. See <a href={`${base}roughness/`}>Rough crack</a> for the kernel
    head-to-head at the centroids of a buried crack,
    <a href={`${base}isosurface/`}>Isosurfaces</a> for the same disagreement
    off the fault, and <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for what
    <i>eps</i> is.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startOnFault } from "../viz/onfault";
    startOnFault(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
